"A note on \"Cryptographically strong permutations from the butterfly structure\". | EBSCOhost (https://openurl.ebsco.com/contentitem/gcd%3A155433704?crl=c&id=ebsco%3Agcd%3A155433704&jrnl=09251022&sid=ebsco%3Aplink%3Acrawler-gcd)\nciteturn9search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # A note on \"Cryptographically strong permutations from the butterfly structure\". ... Published in: Designs, Codes & Cryptography, 2022, v. 90, n. 2, p. 265, doi. ... ## Very recently, a class of cryptographically strong permutations with boomerang uniformity 4 and the best known nonlinearity is constructed from the closed butterfly structure in Li et al.\n\nPeer reviewed|\n\nAcademic Journal\n\n# A note on \"Cryptographically strong permutations from the butterfly structure\".\n\nBy: Li, Nian; Hu, Zhao; Xiong, Maosheng; ... Published in: Designs, Codes & Cryptography, 2022, v. 90, n. 2, p. 265, doi. 10.1007/s10623-021-00974-5\n\n\nAuthors\n    \n\n## Li, Nian; Hu, Zhao; Xiong, Maosheng; Zeng, Xiangyong\n\nAbstract\n    \n\n## Very recently, a class of cryptographically strong permutations with boomerang uniformity 4 and the best known nonlinearity is constructed from the closed butterfly structure in Li et al. (Des Codes Cryptogr 89(4):737–761, 2021). In this note, we provide two additional results concerning these permutations. We first represent the conditions of these permutation obtained in Li et al. (Des Codes Cryptogr 89(4):737–761, 2021) in a much simpler form, and then show that they are linear equivalent to Gold functions. We also prove a criterion for solving a new type of equations over finite fields, which is useful and may be of independent interest.\n\nSubjects\n    \n\n## Permutations; Butterflies; Uniformity; Finite fields\n\nPublication\n    \n\n## Designs, Codes & Cryptography, 2022, Vol 90, Issue 2, p265\n\nISSN\n    \n\n## 0925-1022\n\nPublication type\n    \n\n## Academic Journal\n\nDOI\n    \n\n## 10.1007/s10623-021-00974-5--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpen problems of the 33rd Workshop on Cycles and Colourings (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/397321405_Open_problems_of_the_33rd_Workshop_on_Cycles_and_Colourings/fulltext/690c1287a404d65709a361e2/Open-problems-of-the-33rd-Workshop-on-Cycles-and-Colourings.pdf)\nciteturn9search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Boxed statement: “Conjecture 6.1  Each bridgeless graph admits a 2-flow φ₂ and a 4-flow φ₄ such that for any edge e, if φ₂(e) = 0, then |φ₄(e)| ≥ 2.” ... We have verified this conjecture for all cyclically 4-edge-connected snarks up to 34 vertices, for all snarks with circular flow number 5 on 36 vertices, and for snarks of oddness 4 up to 44 vertices.\n6  A conjecture on a pair of two flows\n\nJOZEF RAJNÍK  \nComenius University, Bratislava, Slovakia  \njozef.rajnik@fmph.uniba.sk\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nBoxed statement: “Conjecture 6.1  Each bridgeless graph admits a 2-flow φ₂ and a 4-flow φ₄ such that for any edge e, if φ₂(e) = 0, then |φ₄(e)| ≥ 2.”\n</visual_element>\n\nThis conjecture implies the 5-flow conjecture since (5φ₂ + φ₄)/2 is a circular 5-flow. Thus, it offers a possible approach to the 5-flow conjecture. It can also be viewed as a generalisation of Seymour’s approach in the proof of the 6-flow theorem, where considering a pair of 2- and 3-flows played a crucial role. Beside forbidding the pair of flow values (0, 0), our setting also forbids pairs (0, ±1). We call this pair of flows a 1/2-flow-pair. We have verified this conjecture for all cyclically 4-edge-connected snarks up to 34 vertices, for all snarks with circular flow number 5 on 36 vertices, and for snarks of oddness 4 up to 44 vertices. Further information on such flow pairs will be available in the upcoming manuscript [1].\n\nReferences\n\n[1] Gáborik, L., Kurz, S., Mazzuoccolo, G., Rajník, J., Rieg, F. Manhattan and Chebyshev flows. Manuscript.\n\n[2] Seymour, P. D. (1981). Nowhere-zero 6-flows. Journal of combinatorial theory, series B, 30 (2), 130–135.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: A note on \"Cryptographically strong permutations from the butterfly structure\". (https://dblp1.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/dcc/LiHXZ22.html)\nciteturn9search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Nian Li et al. (2022) ... A note on \"Cryptographically strong permutations from the butterfly structure\".\n\n# \"A note on \"Cryptographically strong permutations from the butterfly ...\"\n\n  * help us\n\n    * How can I correct errors in dblp?\n    * contact dblp\n\nNian Li et al. (2022)\n\n  *   * Image: use mirror server\n\nTrier\n\n  * Dagstuhl\n\n  * > Home\n\n## Details and statistics\n\n  * show external API response\n\n    * JSON @ openalex.org\n\nsee also:\n\n    * API doc @ openalex.org\n\nDOI: 10.1007/S10623-021-00974-5\n\naccess: closed\n\ntype: Journal Article\n\nmetadata version: 2025-11-19\n\n  * Image: Journal Articles\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI\n      * unpaywalled version\n\n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/dcc/LiHXZ22\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/dcc/LiHXZ22\n\nNian Li, Zhao Hu, Maosheng XiongImage: 0000-0001-6044-9561, Xiangyong Zeng:\nA note on \"Cryptographically strong permutations from the butterfly structure\". Des. Codes Cryptogr. 90(2): 265-276 (2022)\n\n\nPrivacy notice: By enabling the option above, your browser will contact the API of openalex.org to load additional information. Although we do not have any reason to believe that your call will be tracked, we do not 